Revision history for Perl extension Web::Session (formerly Apache::Session::Browseable).

1.0.0 (Web::Session, fork of Apache::Session::Browseable 1.4.0)
    - Fork of Apache::Session::Browseable, renamed Web::Session
      (https://github.com/linagora/perl-Web-Session)
    - Apache::Session::Serialize::JSON and Apache::Session::Generate::SHA256
      renamed Web::Session::Serialize::JSON and Web::Session::Generate::SHA256
    - Postgres is now an alias of PgJSON, MySQL of MySQLJSON; new MariaDB
      alias of MariaDBJSON
    - Drop the Oracle, Informix, Sybase and PgHstore backends
    - New MariaDBJSON backend
    - PgJSON and Postgres: connection pool used by default (MaxIdle,
      IdleTimeout, MaxLifetime; KeepConnections => 0 to disable), one
      connection per tied session, a new session written once
    - PgJSON and Postgres: Commit is on by default when no Handle is given
      (Commit => 0 to roll back at untie)
    - Patroni: uses the same pool, keyed by the primary node, with one
      transaction per tied session (the row read stays locked until untie)
    - PgJSON and Postgres: class methods (searchOn(), deleteIfLowerThan()...)
      borrow a connection of the pool for each call
    - DBI backends reuse their connection by default (noreuse to disable),
      fork safe
    - searchLt() and searchGt() for SQL, Redis and LDAP backends; if the SQL
      comparison fails, sessions are compared in Perl (see "ERRORS" in
      Web::Session)
    - PgJSON: optional GIN index (GinIndex)
    - SQL backends don't rewrite a session stored back unchanged
      (RewriteUnchanged to always write it); the JSON serializer sorts keys
    - Quote table and column names; fix SQL injections through
      deleteIfLowerThan() values and JSON field names
    - deleteIfLowerThan(): fix "and" and "not" rules, invalid rules
    - PostgreSQL: deleteIfLowerThan() deletes by batches
      ($Web::Session::_common::BatchSize)
    - Faster full scans (batches, no eval per row), index documentation
    - JSON serializer: decode UTF-8 bytes, Redis supports characters above
      U+00FF
    - Many Redis fixes (index cleanup, foreign keys, glob characters,
      batched reads)
    - LDAP: deleteIfLowerThan()
